WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:05.263
Vision Two turns football penalty video into structured, reviewable evidence.

2
00:00:05.263 --> 00:00:09.233
This project connects computer vision with human judgement.

3
00:00:10.333 --> 00:00:15.929
The dataset covers thirty-nine penalties from four twenty-eighteen World Cup shoot-outs.

4
00:00:15.929 --> 00:00:22.033
Shared clip identifiers connect model observations, human labels, and final feature tables.

5
00:00:23.133 --> 00:00:26.458
Pretrained YOLO eleven models extract observations.

6
00:00:26.458 --> 00:00:33.662
Optical flow compensates for camera motion, while motion-constrained beam search links ball detections.

7
00:00:33.662 --> 00:00:36.433
Human review resolves uncertain cases.

8
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:42.161
The workflow describes the taker, goalkeeper, ball trajectory, and observed outcome.

9
00:00:42.161 --> 00:00:49.733
Goal labels are kept separate from ball tracking, so a tracking error does not automatically change the result.

10
00:00:50.833 --> 00:00:54.229
All thirty-nine step counts are manually confirmed.

11
00:00:54.229 --> 00:00:58.109
Automatic counting produced thirty-five outputs, with four failures.

12
00:00:58.109 --> 00:01:04.900
Mean absolute error is two point six six steps, so manual review remains essential.

13
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:12.175
Automatic quality checks accepted eighteen goalkeeper records and twenty-six ball trajectories.

14
00:01:12.175 --> 00:01:14.981
The remaining records require review.

15
00:01:14.981 --> 00:01:20.033
These acceptance counts measure quality-control status, not detection accuracy.

16
00:01:21.133 --> 00:01:24.987
The result is an auditable dataset, with explicit uncertainty.

17
00:01:24.987 --> 00:01:32.267
Explore the methods, limitations, and all thirty-nine penalties in the Vision Two case study on my portfolio.
